{
  "id": "0zw2jwa",
  "title": "CHAPTER 7: INDIAN POLITY AND CONSTITUTION",
  "order": 7,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "cdfrxq0",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7.1 Making of the Constitution"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The Indian Constitution was drafted by the Constituent Assembly formed in November 1946, chaired by Dr. Rajendra Prasad. The Drafting Committee was chaired by Dr. B.R. Ambedkar. The Constitution was adopted on 26th November 1949 (Constitution Day) and came into force on 26th January 1950 (Republic Day).",
      "id": "fv8tg9n"
    },
    {
      "id": "f35elil",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Longest written constitution in the world",
        "Originally 395 Articles, 8 Schedules, 22 Parts",
        "Currently: 448 Articles, 12 Schedules, 25 Parts (after amendments)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "w8lzp3a",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7.2 Preamble"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The Preamble to the Indian Constitution declares India to be a Sovereign, Socialist, Secular, Democratic Republic. It secures to all citizens: Justice (social, economic, political), Liberty (thought, expression, belief, faith, worship), Equality (status and opportunity), and Fraternity (dignity and unity). 'Socialist' and 'Secular' were added by the 42nd Amendment (1976) during Emergency.",
      "id": "64eribp"
    },
    {
      "id": "364ddt0",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7.3 Fundamental Rights (Part III, Articles 12–35)"
    },
    {
      "id": "c1a57zq",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Right</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Articles</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Key Provisions</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Right to Equality</p>",
            "<p>14–18</p>",
            "<p>Equality before law, prohibition of discrimination, equality of opportunity in public employment</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Right to Freedom</p>",
            "<p>19–22</p>",
            "<p>6 freedoms (speech, assembly, movement, etc.); right to life; protection against arrest</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Right against Exploitation</p>",
            "<p>23–24</p>",
            "<p>Prohibition of traffic in human beings; prohibition of child labour</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Right to Freedom of Religion</p>",
            "<p>25–28</p>",
            "<p>Freedom of conscience; right to manage religious affairs</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Cultural and Educational Rights</p>",
            "<p>29–30</p>",
            "<p>Protection of language/culture; right to establish educational institutions</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Right to Constitutional Remedies</p>",
            "<p>32</p>",
            "<p>Right to move Supreme Court (Dr. Ambedkar called this 'heart and soul of Constitution')</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ej594is",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7.4 Directive Principles of State Policy (Part IV, Articles 36–51)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "DPSPs are non-justiciable (cannot be enforced by courts) but are fundamental in governance. They require the state to promote welfare, equal pay for equal work, free legal aid, living wages, uniform civil code (Article 44), promotion of cottage industries, and protection of environment.",
      "id": "i4fd0cp"
    },
    {
      "id": "07w94dc",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7.5 Fundamental Duties (Article 51-A)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Added by the 42nd Amendment (1976) on recommendations of Swaran Singh Committee. Originally 10 duties, 11th added by 86th Amendment (2002) – duty of parents to provide education to children. Key duties include: respecting the Constitution, defending the country, protecting the natural environment, upholding the dignity of women.",
      "id": "877mgrc"
    },
    {
      "id": "44pum5x",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
